(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-comorbidities-contraindications-scores-quiz"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Comorbidities, Contraindications & Scores — Quiz 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has 2 weeks of fatigue and a temperature of 38.4 °C (101.1 °F). She has no known heart disease, does not inject drugs and has no skin, eye or nail findings. Three sets of blood cultures show no growth. Transesophageal echocardiography shows an oscillating mass on the mitral valve. Which modified Duke classification fits?","choices":["Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: two major criteria","Possible: three minor criteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The oscillating mass on the valve is a major criterion (1), and a fever of 38.0 °C or higher is a minor criterion (1). Her cultures are negative and diabetes is not a listed predisposing heart condition, so she has one major plus one minor: possible endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25"},{"q":"A 19-year-old basketball player with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with outflow obstruction has a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border. He also has seasonal allergies. Which maneuver will make the murmur louder?","choices":["Valsalva maneuver","Passive leg raising","Sustained handgrip","Rapid squatting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Valsalva lowers preload, and anything that decreases blood flow through the heart increases the outflow obstruction and makes the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy louder. Standing, and amyl nitrite through lowered afterload, do the same. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 68-69"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation, a permanent pacemaker, chronic kidney disease with reduced renal function and an allergy to iodinated contrast has calf claudication and an ankle-brachial index of 0.6. Which imaging study is most appropriate for her?","choices":["Duplex ultrasound","Computed tomography angiography","Conventional angiography","Magnetic resonance angiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasound is the mainstay, initial noninvasive imaging when arterial stenosis is suspected, and it needs no contrast or radiation. Each alternative collides with one of her conditions: contrast allergy and reduced renal function rule out computed tomography angiography, and a pacemaker rules out magnetic resonance angiography. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 46-48"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who smoked for 40 years and has hypertension and type 2 diabetes has a 3.4 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm on screening ultrasound. He has no abdominal or back pain. How often should the aneurysm be imaged?","choices":["Elective repair now","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","No follow-up imaging","Ultrasound every 3 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The infrarenal aorta averages 2 cm and over 3 cm is aneurysmal; a 3.0 to 3.9 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm is followed with ultrasound every three years. Surveillance frequency is set by size, so his diabetes, which is linked with a lower risk of aneurysm, does not change it. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 83, 88, 95"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy, hypertension and type 2 diabetes asks how much sodium he may eat each day. Which daily sodium limit should he follow?","choices":["Less than 1 g","Less than 2 g","Less than 3 g","Less than 4 g"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education in dilated cardiomyopathy includes restricting sodium to less than 2 g per day, together with medication adherence, regular follow-up and removing any offending source such as alcohol or cardiotoxic agents. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes and hyperlipidemia has crushing chest pain. His corrected QT was 420 ms on arrival. The monitor now shows a fast wide-complex rhythm whose QRS shapes vary from beat to beat: polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. He is awake with a pulse and a blood pressure of 108/70 mm Hg. Which is the next step?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia without a prolonged QT is most commonly due to acute myocardial ischemia, as with his crushing chest pain. Even if the patient looks well, polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable: defibrillate, then urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 42, 46"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with hypothyroidism has her third episode of pericarditis. She has stayed on colchicine, but every attempt to taper prednisone has brought a flare (steroid-dependent disease). She has a temperature of 38.4 °C and a C-reactive protein of 42 mg/L (normal under 10). Which therapy is preferred now?","choices":["Stopping colchicine","Higher-dose prednisone","Ibuprofen by itself","Anakinra or rilonacept"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Current guidance supports anti-interleukin-1 therapy (anakinra or rilonacept) earlier for an inflammatory phenotype (C-reactive protein over 10 mg/L, fever, pericardial inflammation), and it is preferred for recurrent, colchicine-resistant or steroid-dependent disease. She is recurrent, steroid-dependent and inflamed. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 35"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man had an upper respiratory infection last week. He has sharp substernal chest pain that is worse lying back and eases leaning forward. No pericardial friction rub (a superficial scratchy sound) is heard on repeated examination. His electrocardiogram shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression and reciprocal ST depression in aVR. Echocardiography shows no pericardial effusion. How many of the four diagnostic criteria for acute pericarditis does he meet?","choices":["Criteria met: 1","Criteria met: 2","Criteria met: 3","Criteria met: 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The four criteria are sharp substernal chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, suggestive electrocardiogram changes (widespread ST elevation) and a new or worsening effusion. He has the chest pain (1) and the electrocardiogram changes (1): 1 + 1 = 2, which meets the two-of-four rule for a clinical diagnosis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 19"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has a temperature of 39.2 °C (102.6 °F) and painless flat red spots on his soles (Janeway lesions). Two separate blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Transthoracic echocardiography shows no vegetation, and a transesophageal study is pending. Which modified Duke classification fits now?","choices":["Definite: two major criteria","Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: three minor criteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus from two separate blood cultures is one major criterion. Injection drug use (predisposition), fever of 38.0 °C or higher, and Janeway lesions (vascular phenomena) are three minor criteria: 1 major + 3 minor = definite endocarditis, even before the transesophageal study. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 22, 23"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man comes to the emergency department with three days of left calf pain. He is receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer, and he also has hypertension and type 2 diabetes and smokes. His left calf measures 4 cm more than the right 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, he has pitting edema confined to the left leg, and he is tender along the course of the deep veins. No other diagnosis seems as likely. What is his Wells score for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer under treatment 1 + calf swelling more than 3 cm over the other leg 1 + pitting edema confined to the symptomatic leg 1 + tenderness along the deep venous system 1 = 4, which is 3 or greater: high likelihood. Hypertension and smoking are deep vein thrombosis risk factors but not Wells items, and diabetes is not scored either. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 49-50, 54-55"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy collapsed while running and was resuscitated from ventricular fibrillation. He also has seasonal allergies and anxiety and is now stable on metoprolol. Which is the most appropriate addition to protect him from sudden death?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","Implanted defibrillator","Alcohol septal ablation","Digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden cardiac death is the leading cause of death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and in more than 80% of cases it comes from ventricular fibrillation, which he has already survived. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator, part of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy management, is placed to treat that rhythm if it returns. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 66, 77, 79, 81-82"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects heroin and has hepatitis C has tricuspid valve endocarditis. Blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which antibiotic fits?","choices":["Vancomycin","Nafcillin","Cefazolin","Penicillin G"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus causes most endocarditis in people who inject drugs, and 80 to 90% when the tricuspid valve is infected. For the methicillin-RESISTANT strain, the listed agents are vancomycin or daptomycin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease and diabetes has missed a week of dialysis. He has pleuritic chest pain (sharp, worse with each breath in) and a pericardial friction rub (a superficial scratchy sound). Blood urea nitrogen is 96 mg/dL (normal 7-20). Echocardiography shows a small effusion without tamponade. Which treatment addresses the cause?","choices":["Pericardiectomy","Resume dialysis","Tuberculosis therapy","Prednisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL marks uremic pericarditis, and his 96 after a missed week of dialysis fits. Treatment targets the underlying disorder, and for uremia that is dialysis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 34"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, hip osteoarthritis and a 30-pack-year smoking history has pain, numbness and weakness in both thighs when he walks. Standing still does not relieve it; the symptoms ease only when he sits down or leans forward. His pedal pulses are normal. Which is the most likely cause of his leg symptoms?","choices":["Hip osteoarthritis","Spinal stenosis","Diabetic peripheral neuropathy","Aortoiliac artery disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spinal stenosis causes claudication-like symptoms with numbness and weakness that are relieved by rest AND by sitting down or leaning forward to straighten the lumbar spine. Rest alone relieves true claudication, and his normal pulses argue against arterial disease despite his diabetes and smoking. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 14, 19, 40-41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man treated with chest radiation for lymphoma 15 years ago has anasarca (generalized swelling) and an elevated jugular venous pressure that rises during inspiration (Kussmaul sign). Echocardiography shows a thickened pericardium with septal bounce: constrictive pericarditis. Pericardiectomy is scheduled for next week. How should his diuretics be handled before surgery?","choices":["Add a second diuretic","Increase them aggressively","Swap them for fluid boluses","Use them sparingly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis is often a surgical disease, and if surgery is planned, diuretics should be used sparingly in the preoperative period. Diuretics treat fluid overload in early, stable constriction on a medical trial, but heavy preoperative diuresis is avoided. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 41, 49"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man being evaluated for endocarditis has painful red nodules on his finger pads, painless flat red spots on his soles, conjunctival hemorrhages, and septic pulmonary infarcts on chest imaging. Which finding counts as an immunologic minor criterion?","choices":["Septic pulmonary infarcts","Painful finger-pad nodules","Painless spots on the soles","Conjunctival hemorrhages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Painful finger-pad nodules are Osler nodes, and the immunologic minor criterion covers glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots and rheumatoid factor. His other three findings belong to the VASCULAR minor criterion, so together he has two separate minor criteria from these signs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 22"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with hypertension had a myocardial infarction 3 weeks ago and takes aspirin. She now has fever, sharp pleuritic chest pain (worse with each breath in) that eases leaning forward, and widespread concave ST elevation: Dressler syndrome. A colchicine-based regimen with gastrointestinal protection is planned. Which drug should be avoided?","choices":["Indomethacin","A proton pump inhibitor","Aspirin","Colchicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis weeks after an infarction (Dressler syndrome) is post-infarction pericarditis, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as indomethacin and ibuprofen are not given for it; aspirin is the anti-inflammatory used instead, with colchicine and gastrointestinal protection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 28, 29, 34"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a prior myocardial infarction and type 2 diabetes collapsed from ventricular fibrillation and was resuscitated with defibrillation. He is now stable, and no reversible cause was found. Before discharge, which is recommended?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker alone","Ablation of early beats","Implantable defibrillator","Beta blocker without a device"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized after life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, most patients need an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death. Coronary artery disease, as in his prior infarction, is the most common predisposing condition for sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 37, 38, 45"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has a first deep vein thrombosis of the calf confirmed by duplex ultrasound. She is hemodynamically stable, has no bleeding history and lives with her daughter. She also has type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease, with a creatinine clearance of 24 mL/min (normal above 90 mL/min). Which treatment plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Outpatient apixaban monotherapy","Admit for heparin infusion and warfarin","Inferior vena cava filter instead","Outpatient enoxaparin, then dabigatran"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min means she must be hospitalized to start treatment: intravenous unfractionated heparin as a bolus and continuous infusion, adjusted by activated partial thromboplastin time, overlapped with warfarin for at least 5 days and until the international normalized ratio is above 2 for 24 hours. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 62, 65-67, 69"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man with asthma went hiking 3 weeks ago, had a tick bite and then an expanding red rash. He now feels lightheaded. His electrocardiogram shows Wenckebach block (the PR interval grows longer with each beat until a QRS is dropped). He takes no medications and has no history of heart disease. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Age-related degeneration","Ischemia after infarction","A medication effect","Lyme carditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A PR interval that lengthens until a QRS drops is second-degree Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block, and Lyme disease (Lyme carditis) is a listed cause. A tick bite and expanding rash 3 weeks before, in a young man on no medications, point to it rather than age-related or ischemic causes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 7, 9"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player's father was just diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy after fainting. The teen has no symptoms and uses an inhaler for exercise-induced asthma. Which step is most appropriate for the teen?","choices":["Implanted defibrillator","Genetic screening","Start metoprolol","Wait for symptoms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is mostly inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, so one affected parent is enough to pass it on, and evaluation is often prompted by disease in a family member. Genetic screening is the care strategy named for adolescents. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 58-59, 64, 105"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with generalized anxiety drinks six cups of coffee a day. A 24-hour monitor done for an insurance physical shows premature ventricular contractions making up 0.5% of beats (about 700 per day). Her echocardiogram is normal, she has never fainted and she has no symptoms. Which management fits her risk group?","choices":["Reassure; remove triggers","Catheter ablation","Beta blocker first line","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A burden under 1% (or 1,000 a day), no structural heart disease and no symptoms puts her in the low-risk, asymptomatic group, managed with reassurance and eliminating triggers. Caffeine is a listed trigger, so cutting her coffee fits. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man was started on dabigatran after 5 days of enoxaparin for a deep vein thrombosis. He also has hypertension and atrial fibrillation. He now has an acute intracranial hemorrhage after a fall. Which agent reverses his anticoagulant?","choices":["Prothrombin complex concentrate","Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dabigatran, the direct thrombin inhibitor, has its own reversal agent, idarucizumab, for life-threatening or intracranial hemorrhage. The enoxaparin he received earlier is no longer the drug on board; the agent he takes now decides the antidote. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 61-62, 68"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man comes to the emergency department 3 weeks after a total hip replacement. His entire left leg is swollen, with pitting edema confined to that leg, and his left calf measures 5 cm more than the right. He also has heart failure and type 2 diabetes. No alternative diagnosis is likely. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassess in one week","Contrast venography","D-dimer before imaging","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Major surgery within 12 weeks 1 + entire leg swollen 1 + pitting edema 1 + calf swelling more than 3 cm 1 = 4, high likelihood. A high score goes straight to ultrasound of the thigh, with or without a D-dimer, and venous duplex ultrasound has replaced venography for the diagnosis. Heart failure raises his risk but adds no Wells point. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 54-57"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, obesity and a 40-pack-year smoking history has worsening dyspnea on exertion. His B-type natriuretic peptide is 62 pg/mL (reference below 100 pg/mL). How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Filling pressure is elevated","Heart failure is unlikely","Heart failure is likely","Restrictive disease is confirmed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide is released from the ventricles when filling pressures are high, and a level below 100 pg/mL is the cutoff used to rule heart failure out, so heart failure is unlikely. At 62 pg/mL his breathlessness is unlikely to be cardiac, which turns attention back to his lung disease and smoking. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 40, 96-97"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a transcatheter aortic valve replacement and chronic kidney disease had anaphylaxis to penicillin as a child. She needs a tooth extraction and can swallow pills. Which prophylaxis regimen fits?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral clindamycin","Oral azithromycin","Oral cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A transcatheter prosthetic valve qualifies for prophylaxis before an extraction. A patient allergic to penicillin who can take pills gets azithromycin or clarithromycin (or doxycycline), and because her allergy was anaphylaxis, cephalexin is not an option for her. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with hypertension is found to have atrial fibrillation. She has no heart failure, diabetes, stroke, transient ischemic attack or vascular disease. What is her CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score?","choices":["Score of 1","Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age 63 is under 65 and scores 0, female sex adds 1 and hypertension 1: 0 + 1 + 1 = 2. The age points start at 65, which is the usual trap in a patient in her early sixties. Two points for a woman is above the low-risk line of 1. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and hypertension has a 4.1 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm on surveillance. For three days he has had constant low back pain without any injury. He is hemodynamically stable. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repair once over 5.5 cm","Imaging in 6 to 12 months","Analgesia and reassurance","Urgent vascular referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain from an aneurysm calls for urgent vascular referral regardless of its size, because pain occurs with aneurysmal expansion. A symptomatic unruptured aneurysm is managed with pain and blood pressure control and surgical repair unless contraindicated, so his 4.1 cm size does not allow waiting. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 89, 92, 95, 100"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with outflow obstruction has exertional chest pressure. Coronary angiography last year showed normal arteries. He also has migraine headaches and mild hypertension. Which medication is the most appropriate initial choice for his symptoms?","choices":["Metoprolol","Furosemide","Digoxin","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker such as metoprolol is the initial medication for outflow tract obstruction: a negative chronotrope and inotrope that slows the heart, improves diastolic filling and lowers contractility. His angina without coronary disease comes from the obstruction and the oxygen demand of hypertrophied muscle. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 66, 76-77"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman treated 15 years ago with chemotherapy and mediastinal radiation for lymphoma has progressive dyspnea, ascites, leg edema and jugular veins that rise with inspiration (Kussmaul sign). Echocardiography shows a preserved ejection fraction, normal ventricular size and markedly enlarged atria. Which test best differentiates restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Posteroanterior chest radiograph","Repeat transthoracic echocardiogram","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her radiation and chemotherapy can both cause restrictive cardiomyopathy, and constrictive pericarditis mimics it, with Kussmaul sign possible in either. Echocardiography alone is not definitive because the two look alike; cardiac magnetic resonance imaging differentiates them by showing pericardial thickening in pericarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 87-88, 92, 94-97"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted with takotsubo cardiomyopathy after a car accident has apical hypokinesis on echocardiography, and a repeat study shows a left ventricular thrombus. She also has hypertension and osteoarthritis and is hemodynamically stable without heart failure. Which addition to her care is most appropriate?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Transplant evaluation","Diuretic therapy","Endomyocardial biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo treatment is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation: beta blockers are helpful, anticoagulation is added if a left ventricular thrombus is present, and diuretics are used if heart failure is present. Her thrombus calls for anticoagulation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 44, 55, 82, 103-104"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with right-sided weakness from a stroke two months ago lives at home and comes to the emergency department with swelling of his right calf; his thigh is not swollen. He has been confined to bed for the past 5 days with a chest cold. His right calf measures 4 cm more than the left, there is pitting edema only in the right leg, and collateral superficial veins that are not varicose are visible over the right shin. He also has hypertension and heart failure. No alternative diagnosis is likely. What is his Wells score?","choices":["Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Paresis of the leg 1 + bedridden more than 3 days 1 + calf swelling more than 3 cm 1 + pitting edema confined to the leg 1 + collateral nonvaricose superficial veins 1 = 5, high likelihood. His hypertension and heart failure are deep vein thrombosis risk factors but earn no Wells points. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 49-50, 54-55"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with heart failure and alcohol use disorder has recurrent runs of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS complexes twist around the baseline (torsades de pointes), with a corrected QT of 540 ms. He stays conscious with a blood pressure of 104/66 mm Hg and is already on a magnesium infusion. An antiarrhythmic is to be added. Which one can be used?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous lidocaine","Carvedilol by mouth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In torsades de pointes amiodarone and procainamide are avoided because they prolong the QT, and beta blockers are avoided because their bradycardia indirectly prolongs it. Intravenous lidocaine is the antiarrhythmic that CAN be used, although the evidence for it is limited; magnesium remains the core treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 43, 44, 47"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has type 2 diabetes, varicose veins and a long smoking history. She has a painful, dry, punched-out ulcer on the tip of her great toe that bleeds very little when cleaned, and her foot is cool with shiny, hairless skin. There is no swelling, blistering or drainage. Which type of ulcer is this most likely?","choices":["Arterial ischemic ulcer","Venous stasis ulcer","Wet gangrene","Erythema induratum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic ulcers form at the ends of arterial branches, such as the tips of the toes, and are dry, punched out, painful and bleed little. Her cool, shiny, hairless foot fits severely reduced blood flow; her varicose veins do not make this a venous ulcer. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 28, 37, 39; CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 15, 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with hypertension and type 2 diabetes is found to have non-valvular atrial fibrillation. She has no heart failure, stroke, transient ischemic attack, myocardial infarction, peripheral artery disease or aortic plaque. What is her CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score?","choices":["Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age 70 falls in the 65-74 band for 1 point, female sex adds 1, hypertension 1 and diabetes 1: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4. She has no heart failure, no prior stroke or transient ischemic attack (2 points) and no vascular disease, so nothing else is added. A score this high means she typically needs anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman treated with chemotherapy for breast cancer three years ago has progressive dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness when lying flat) and a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound). She also has hypothyroidism. Echocardiography shows all four chambers dilated with thin walls, global hypokinesis and an ejection fraction of 20%. Which cardiomyopathy is most likely?","choices":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chemotherapy can cause both dilated and restrictive cardiomyopathy, so the echocardiogram decides: dilated, thin-walled, hypokinetic chambers with a reduced ejection fraction are dilated cardiomyopathy. Her third heart sound reflects a large volume of blood striking a very compliant, volume-overloaded ventricle. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 23, 30, 38, 59, 72, 85-87, 101, 103"},{"q":"A 20-year-old college soccer player with mild asthma was diagnosed with viral pericarditis 2 weeks ago. His pain has been gone for 5 days, but his C-reactive protein is still 18 mg/L (normal under 10). He asks when he can return to full training. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["After 24 hours free of symptoms","After a week of colchicine therapy","Now, because his pain has resolved","Once 1 month out and markers normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous activity is avoided until symptoms resolve and biomarkers such as C-reactive protein normalize, and for at least 1 month after diagnosis with a maximal heart rate under 100. At 2 weeks with a C-reactive protein of 18 he meets neither the time nor the marker condition. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 28, 30"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a known 4.8 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm needs a study that defines its size and extent more precisely than ultrasound. He is hemodynamically stable and has a documented allergy to iodinated contrast dye. Which study is most appropriate?","choices":["Abdominal radiograph","Computed tomography angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Computed tomography scan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance imaging is comparable to computed tomography for defining an aneurysm, without exposure to dye or ionizing radiation, and it is useful in stable patients with a dye allergy. He is stable and allergic to contrast, which makes it the right study. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 47, 94, 96-97"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with a prior myocardial infarction has monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at 180 beats per minute. Her blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg, she is alert and oriented and has no chest pain, but she is short of breath. A pulse is present. Which is the next step?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Unsynchronized defibrillation","Monitor; she is stable","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Instability in ventricular tachycardia is defined as ANY one of chest pain, altered mental status, shortness of breath or hypotension. Her shortness of breath alone makes her unstable despite a normal pressure, and with a pulse present unstable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia gets synchronized cardioversion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man with mild asthma had a flulike illness two weeks ago and now has new heart failure that keeps worsening despite medical treatment. Coronary angiography is normal, and other causes of heart failure have been excluded. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Serum troponin","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Endomyocardial biopsy","Chest radiograph"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard for myocarditis. It is used once other causes of heart failure are excluded and is recommended when heart function deteriorates acutely from an unknown cause despite medical treatment, as his does after a normal angiogram. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 51, 54-55, 103"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with hypertension and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a 5.2 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm on surveillance ultrasound. She has no pain and is hemodynamically stable. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Ultrasound every 6 to 12 months","Ultrasound every 3 years","Elective repair","Repair once over 5.5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elective repair is recommended at 5.0 cm or larger in women, a lower threshold than the more-than-5.5 cm used for men, and women with aneurysms rupture more often. Her 5.2 cm aneurysm has crossed that line; endovascular or open repair is chosen with her age and comorbidities in mind. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 88, 95, 101"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with non-valvular atrial fibrillation, hypertension and type 2 diabetes has a stroke-risk score that calls for anticoagulation and a low bleeding-risk score. Which anticoagulant choice is preferred?","choices":["A direct oral anticoagulant","Aspirin as the sole agent","Warfarin, ratio goal 3 to 4","No anticoagulant needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a stroke-risk score that calls for anticoagulation and a low bleeding risk he should be anticoagulated, and in non-valvular atrial fibrillation the direct oral anticoagulants (dabigatran, apixaban, edoxaban or rivaroxaban) are preferred. Warfarin, if used, targets an international normalized ratio of 2 to 3. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman comes to urgent care the day after she felt a sudden pull in her right calf while sprinting. The calf is painful, with bruising near the ankle, and she is tender along the deep veins of the calf. The calf is 1 cm larger than the left, with no edema. She takes an estrogen-containing oral contraceptive. A calf muscle tear is judged at least as likely as a deep vein thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Empiric anticoagulation","Contrast venography","D-dimer blood test","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Deep-vein tenderness 1, minus 2 for an alternative diagnosis at least as likely (a calf muscle tear, with its inciting injury and bruising at the ankle), gives minus 1: low likelihood. Low likelihood starts with a D-dimer; a negative result stops the workup and a positive one leads to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 53-57"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man who injects drugs is being treated for aortic valve infective endocarditis. On day 6 he becomes dizzy. His electrocardiogram shows regular P waves at 80 per minute and a separate regular ventricular rhythm at 36, with no relationship between the P waves and the QRS complexes. He takes no rate-slowing drugs and reports no tick bites. Which is the most likely cause of the block?","choices":["Lyme carditis infection","Age-related degeneration","Endocarditis with an abscess","A rate-slowing medication"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two unrelated regular rates with a ventricular rate under 40 is third-degree (complete) block, and bacterial endocarditis with an associated abscess is a listed cause. In a man on day 6 of treatment for aortic valve endocarditis, the infection extending into the conduction tissue is the cause that fits. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 15, 17, 18"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a prior stroke has left calf cramping with walking. Systolic pressures are: right arm 118 mm Hg, left arm 146 mm Hg; left ankle posterior tibial 70 and dorsalis pedis 76 mm Hg; right ankle posterior tibial 128 and dorsalis pedis 120 mm Hg. What is her overall ankle-brachial index?","choices":["0.48","0.52","0.64","0.88"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Use the higher arm pressure, 146, as the denominator for both legs and the higher ankle pressure of each leg as the numerator: left 76/146 = 0.52, right 128/146 = 0.88. The lower leg value, 0.52, is her overall index, in the 0.4 to 0.9 claudication band that fits her calf cramping. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 27, 42-43"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation found at a routine visit. She has no hypertension, diabetes, heart failure, stroke, transient ischemic attack or vascular disease, so her CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score is 1, from female sex. Her rate is controlled. What does this score mean for her?","choices":["Low stroke risk; may not need anticoagulation","Low bleeding risk; anticoagulation is safe","Raised stroke risk; needs anticoagulation","Rate control is no longer needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The score estimates stroke risk, and 0 for a man or 1 for a woman is low risk: such a patient may not require anticoagulation. Her single point comes only from female sex, so she sits on that low-risk line. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 49, 50"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with diabetes, chronic kidney disease and a 50-pack-year smoking history wakes at night with burning pain in her right forefoot that she relieves by hanging the leg over the side of the bed. A dry, punched-out ulcer sits on the tip of her second toe. Her right ankle-brachial index is 0.28 (normal above 0.9). Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Arterial revascularization","Cilostazol and observation","Graduated compression stockings","Supervised exercise program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Night pain in the forefoot relieved by dangling the leg, a dry punched-out toe ulcer and an index of 0.28, inside the 0 to 0.4 band of rest pain and tissue loss, mark critical limb ischemia. This limb needs its blood supply restored; a bypass graft is used principally for severe critical leg ischemia. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 21, 23-24, 37, 42, 51, 53, 57; CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Two days after a cardiac catheterization through the right groin, a 70-year-old woman has a new thrill (palpable vibration) and a bruit heard in both systole and diastole over the puncture site. She has chronic kidney disease with reduced renal function and an allergy to iodinated contrast. Which test is most appropriate to confirm the suspected arteriovenous fistula?","choices":["Catheter angiography","Computed tomography angiography","Duplex ultrasound","D-dimer blood test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasound confirms the diagnosis in a suspected iatrogenic arteriovenous fistula, the kind that follows groin access for a procedure, and it uses no contrast or radiation. That matters for her, because contrast allergy and decreased renal function are contraindications to computed tomography angiography. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 57, 82-85; Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 46-48"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with pancreatic cancer has a tender, red, indurated cord along a varicose great saphenous vein in his thigh. Duplex ultrasound shows a long clot in the superficial vein with a thickened wall, and the deep veins are clear. He has no bleeding history. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Warm compresses and elevation alone","Inferior vena cava filter","Vein stripping surgery","Anticoagulation with enoxaparin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Superficial thrombophlebitis is usually treated with warm compresses, compression, elevation and anti-inflammatory drugs, but an extensive clot burden or an intermediate or higher risk of thrombosis calls for anticoagulation with unfractionated heparin, enoxaparin or fondaparinux. His long clot and malignancy qualify. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 43-44, 69, 75-79"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man with heart failure and type 2 diabetes has atrial fibrillation at 150 beats per minute. He has crackles to both lung apices from pulmonary edema, and his blood pressure is 78/46 mm Hg. Which is the next step?","choices":["Cardioversion after 21 days","Oral rate control","Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation with severe heart failure (pulmonary edema) and hypotension is a medical emergency and hemodynamically unstable. Unstable atrial fibrillation gets synchronized cardioversion, not a trial of rate-control drugs. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 48, 49"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve has a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and painful red nodules on her finger pads (Osler nodes). Three sets of blood cultures show no growth, and transesophageal echocardiography shows no vegetation, abscess or new regurgitation. Which modified Duke classification fits?","choices":["Possible: three minor criteria","Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: five minor criteria","Definite: one major, three minor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her prosthetic valve (predisposition), fever of 38.0 °C or higher, and Osler nodes (immunologic phenomena) are three minor criteria, and she has no major criterion. Three minor criteria make possible endocarditis, and a normal transesophageal study does not necessarily rule it out. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 22, 23, 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease has a baseline corrected QT of 520 ms. She develops a regular wide-complex tachycardia at 160 beats per minute with identical QRS complexes. She is alert with a blood pressure of 126/78 mm Hg and has no chest pain, confusion or shortness of breath. Which antiarrhythmic avoids adding to her QT prolongation?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous lidocaine","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She has stable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, treated with lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide. Procainamide is avoided with a prolonged QT, and amiodarone and procainamide both prolong the QT; lidocaine is the antiarrhythmic that CAN be used when the QT is the problem, as it is in torsades. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 44, 47"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure is admitted in respiratory failure from an exacerbation. The monitor shows an irregular rhythm at 128 beats per minute with at least three different P-wave shapes in the same lead and one P wave for every narrow QRS. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 132/78 mm Hg. Which is the priority in managing this rhythm?","choices":["Catheter ablation of foci","Synchronized cardioversion","Adenosine to stop the rhythm","Treat the underlying illness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At least three P-wave shapes in one lead with an irregular rhythm is multifocal atrial tachycardia, which usually comes from severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or heart failure when the patient is acutely ill. Its management is treating the underlying condition; she is stable, so no shock is needed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 28, 31, 32"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes had his mitral valve repaired with an annuloplasty ring 5 years ago. He is scheduled for a dental cleaning that will manipulate his gums. He has no allergies and can take pills. Which is correct about endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Give oral amoxicillin before","Not needed: cleaning is minor","Not needed: the repair is old","Give it after, if he bleeds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prosthetic material used for valve repair, such as annuloplasty rings, is a listed indication, and dental cleaning that manipulates the gums is a listed procedure. With no allergy and an oral route, a single dose of amoxicillin 30 to 60 minutes before is the standard regimen. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 36, 42"},{"q":"A 22-year-old competitive rower with well-controlled seasonal allergies has a preparticipation electrocardiogram showing a regular rhythm at 46 beats per minute with narrow QRS complexes and inverted P waves buried in or just after the QRS. He has no symptoms, takes no medications and his examination is normal. Which is the next step?","choices":["Daily metoprolol","Digoxin immune Fab","Permanent pacemaker","Reassure; no treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is a junctional rhythm (rate 40-60, narrow QRS, inverted P waves around the QRS). It can be seen in healthy patients with increased vagal tone such as well-conditioned athletes, is generally well tolerated, and needs no treatment when the patient is asymptomatic and otherwise healthy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 62, 64"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with hypertension has had continuous atrial fibrillation for 2 years. Two cardioversions failed, and she and her cardiologist have agreed to stop trying to restore sinus rhythm and to focus on rate control. Which category describes her atrial fibrillation now?","choices":["Persistent","Long-standing persistent","Permanent","Paroxysmal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation becomes permanent when the patient and clinician make a joint decision to stop further attempts to restore or maintain sinus rhythm. That decision, not the 2-year duration alone, is what places her in this category. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with peripheral artery disease and type 2 diabetes still smokes a pack a day despite counseling. He takes simvastatin, and his low-density lipoprotein cholesterol is 92 mg/dL. Which low-density lipoprotein target applies to him?","choices":["Below 55 mg/dL","Below 70 mg/dL","Below 100 mg/dL","Below 130 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipid-lowering therapy in peripheral artery disease targets a low-density lipoprotein below 100 mg/dL, and below 70 mg/dL for high-risk patients such as those with diabetes or continued smoking. He has both, so his 92 mg/dL is still above target. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy from amyloidosis has had two episodes of syncope. He also has chronic kidney disease and atrial fibrillation treated with anticoagulation. An electrocardiogram shows low QRS voltage (small complexes) and complete heart block. Which treatment addresses the cause of his syncope?","choices":["Stopping anticoagulation","Increased diuretic dose","Permanent pacemaker","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, fibrosis of the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes can lead to heart block, and a permanent pacemaker treats that conduction system disease. His complete heart block explains the syncope, while his anticoagulation against atrial thrombi continues. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 95, 98-99"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman with osteoporosis and a hip fracture last year has acute idiopathic pericarditis. Her clinician reviews the drugs used for pericarditis with her age and bones in mind. Which drug most raises her osteoporosis risk?","choices":["Colchicine","Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Prednisone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In elderly patients with pericarditis, corticosteroids increase osteoporosis risk, which matters for an 82-year-old with osteoporosis and a prior fracture. Older adults also need caution with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and colchicine, with dose reduction considered, but the bone risk belongs to the corticosteroid. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 36"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic heart failure had a transient ischemic attack last year and now has atrial fibrillation. He has no hypertension, diabetes, myocardial infarction or peripheral artery disease. What is his CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score?","choices":["Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age 75 or older scores 2, heart failure 1, and a prior transient ischemic attack 2 (stroke, transient ischemic attack or thromboembolism count double): 2 + 1 + 2 = 5. Male sex adds nothing, and he has no hypertension, diabetes or vascular disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with a first deep vein thrombosis of the calf is hemodynamically stable, has normal kidney function and no bleeding history, and will be treated at home. He has a severe fear of needles, refuses all injections and cannot return for blood test monitoring. Which anticoagulant fits him best?","choices":["Warfarin","Apixaban","Dabigatran","Edoxaban"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban, like rivaroxaban, is an anti-Xa agent given as oral monotherapy: a loading dose followed by a maintenance dose, with no parenteral lead-in. Of these four it is the only option that starts without any injection, which suits a stable outpatient with normal kidney function. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 61-62, 66"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and exertional dyspnea stopped metoprolol because of intolerable fatigue. She also has hypertension and migraine. Which medication is the best replacement to relieve her outflow obstruction?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Isosorbide mononitrate","Amlodipine","Verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When the beta blocker cannot be used, a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker such as verapamil or diltiazem treats outflow tract obstruction: it is a negative chronotrope and inotrope, slowing the heart and reducing contractility as metoprolol did. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 76-77; CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman whose hyperthyroidism is now controlled has recurrent episodes of abrupt palpitations. During one, the rhythm is regular at 170 beats per minute with one consistently abnormal P wave before every narrow QRS. Adenosine slowed it only briefly without ending it. She stays stable, and the episodes keep returning. Which is the initial chronic therapy?","choices":["Oral beta blocker","Intravenous procainamide","Permanent pacemaker","Repeat adenosine daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia with one consistent abnormal P wave before each QRS that adenosine does not end is focal atrial tachycardia; adenosine is a useful diagnostic tool because it is less effective here than in nodal or atrioventricular reentry. Initial chronic therapy is an oral beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, with ablation usually curative. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 28, 30, 31"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension who still smokes has an abdominal aortic aneurysm that measured 4.1 cm six months ago and measures 4.7 cm today. He has no pain. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Repeat imaging in 6 to 12 months","Repair once over 5.5 cm","Ultrasound every 3 years","Repair the aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Growth of 0.6 cm in six months exceeds the rapid-growth mark of 0.5 cm in six months, and an aneurysm showing rapid growth should be repaired. His continued smoking and hypertension also raise his rupture risk. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 95, 99"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman who receives long-term intravenous nutrition through a central line has fever and a large vegetation on her native tricuspid valve. Blood cultures grow Candida, and antifungal therapy has been started. Which additional step is indicated?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery referral","Heparin anticoagulation","Two weeks of oral antifungal","Adding gentamicin for synergy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infection is a listed indication for surgery because it is difficult to cure with medications alone, and cardiothoracic surgery is consulted to evaluate surgery. Fungal endocarditis also carries a high risk of embolism to large peripheral arteries. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 26, 32, 33, 37"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with coronary artery disease and type 2 diabetes has a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 210 beats per minute with no visible P waves. He has chest pain and is confused, with a blood pressure of 76/44 mm Hg. Which is the next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuver first","Unsynchronized shock","Intravenous adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow-complex tachycardia over 160 with hidden P waves is supraventricular tachycardia, and chest pain, confusion and a pressure of 76/44 make it hemodynamically unstable. Unstable supraventricular tachycardia gets immediate synchronized cardioversion, a timed shock with decreased voltage. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and chronic heart failure has a regular wide-complex tachycardia at 170 beats per minute with identical QRS complexes: monomorphic ventricular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74 mm Hg and has no chest pain or shortness of breath. Which should be avoided in his initial management?","choices":["Supplemental oxygen","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous lidocaine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He is stable (no chest pain, altered mental status, shortness of breath or hypotension), so pharmacologic cardioversion with lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide is the route, but procainamide is avoided in a patient with a prolonged QT or congestive heart failure, and he has heart failure. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who stopped her anticoagulant has had a cold, pale right leg for four hours. Her numbness extends above the toes over the dorsum of the foot, she has pain at rest, and toe and ankle movement are mildly weak. The arterial Doppler signal is inaudible, and the venous Doppler signal is audible. Which Rutherford class fits her limb?","choices":["Class I","Class IIA","Class IIB","Class III"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensory loss reaching beyond the toes with rest pain, plus mild-to-moderate muscle weakness, defines class IIB, immediately threatened: salvageable only with immediate revascularization. An inaudible arterial and audible venous Doppler signal fits that class, and atrial fibrillation is a classic cardiac source of the embolus. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 60-65"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with type 1 diabetes and a family history of early coronary disease develops new heart failure two weeks after a flulike illness. Troponin is elevated, and echocardiography shows global hypokinesis. Myocarditis is suspected. Which test rules out coronary ischemia as the cause of his new heart failure?","choices":["Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac angiography","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Magnetic resonance imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In suspected myocarditis, cardiac angiography is used to rule out coronary ischemia as the cause of new-onset heart failure. His diabetes and family history are cardiac risk factors, which makes ischemic disease a real alternative to exclude before the failure is blamed on his recent viral illness. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 51, 54-55, 102"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension and obesity has had stable atrial fibrillation for 3 months. Her rate is controlled on metoprolol and she is not anticoagulated. She asks to have an outpatient cardioversion to get back into sinus rhythm. Which must come first?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Stopping her metoprolol","Immediate cardioversion","21 days of anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient cardioversion of atrial fibrillation is done only after 21 days of anticoagulation. She is stable and rate controlled, so there is no reason for an urgent shock, and she has had no anticoagulation yet. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"}]};
